# Per-validator settings for yamale-feeder.service. Install at
# /etc/yamale/feeder.env.
#
# FEEDER_SOURCE is the one worth thinking about. The oracle takes a
# stake-weighted median, so a median of four reports drawn from one endpoint is
# one source counted four times — set a DIFFERENT source on each validator, or
# the median is decorative. Today: erapi (open.er-api.com) and yahoo
# (finance.yahoo.com).
#
# FEEDER_KEY is the hot key nominated with `tx oracle delegate-feeder`, never the
# validator's operator key. That is the entire point of the delegation: a
# compromised feeder can report a wrong price and cannot touch the stake.
#
# "Cannot touch the stake" is the whole claim, and it is narrower than it reads.
# It says nothing about what else the same key might hold. On this chain the
# feeder address was also the admin of a funded treasury, which meant a key
# documented as disposable, sitting in an unencrypted keyring on a validator
# host, could rewrite that treasury's spend policy and then empty it -- the
# 50 YML per-spend cap is not a bound against the admin, because SetSpendPolicy
# is an admin-only message.
#
# So the rule is two rules:
#
#   * the feeder key holds nothing and administers nothing. Before nominating
#     one, check: `blockchaind query treasury role-holders --address <feeder>`
#     and `query bank balances <feeder>`.
#   * FEEDER_KEYRING is `file` or `os`, never `test`. Operators copy this file
#     verbatim, and `test` is an unencrypted keyring on disk. The service needs
#     the passphrase at start: put it in a systemd credential or a root-owned
#     file read by ExecStartPre, not in this file.

FEEDER_BINARY=/opt/yamale/bin/blockchaind
FEEDER_HOME=/opt/yamale/node
FEEDER_NODE=http://localhost:26657
FEEDER_CHAIN_ID=yamale-devnet-2
FEEDER_KEYRING=file
FEEDER_KEY=alice
FEEDER_VALIDATOR=ymlvaloper1m9xhc6zy7fxfax9t5fnykh9k2e29faj7p4h3kh
FEEDER_SOURCE=erapi
FEEDER_INTERVAL=60s
